# CODEOWNERS — hardening-backlog item 1, the GitHub half.
#
# ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
# │ OWNER IS @Jnesspace, and that is a known limitation, not the target.     │
# │                                                                          │
# │ `Jnesspace` is a GitHub *user*, not an organisation (verified via the     │
# │ API). Team handles like @Jnesspace/platform-team only resolve inside an   │
# │ organisation, so a team handle here resolves to nothing — and GitHub      │
# │ treats an unresolvable owner as *no owner*, which makes "Require review   │
# │ from Code Owners" silently pass. That fails open, so this file names the  │
# │ user directly instead.                                                   │
# │                                                                          │
# │ Consequence: this file cannot deliver separation of duties today.         │
# │ That control needs two humans — a sole maintainer cannot approve their    │
# │ own PR, so requiring code-owner review in a single-maintainer repo        │
# │ deadlocks every merge instead of gating it.                              │
# │                                                                          │
# │ Target state: move this repo into an organisation, create a real          │
# │ platform team with write access, swap @Jnesspace for @<org>/platform-team │
# │ below, and only then enable "Require review from Code Owners".            │
# │ Until then, enable the solo-safe rules in .github/BRANCH_PROTECTION.md    │
# │ (required status checks, no force-push, linear history) — those are real  │
# │ enforcement that does not need a second reviewer.                        │
# │                                                                          │
# │ Verify owners resolve: gh api repos/Jnesspace/platform-engineering/\      │
# │   codeowners/errors   → must return []                                    │
# └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
#
# Why this file is the highest-value control in the repo: the elevated engine
# stacks (iam-factory, nonadmin-launcher, app-factory) execute whatever is on
# their tracked branch, holding Space-admin. There is no gate between "merged"
# and "run with elevated credentials", so the review gate *is* the privilege
# boundary. An unreviewed merge is a privilege escalation.
#
# CODEOWNERS alone enforces nothing. It only takes effect with branch protection
# requiring code-owner review — see .github/BRANCH_PROTECTION.md.
#
# Last matching pattern wins, so this file reads general → specific.

# Default owner. This is a platform-team repo end to end; product teams consume
# it from their own repos and never commit here.
*                       @Jnesspace

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The lines below are deliberately redundant against the `*` default. They exist
# so that narrowing the default later cannot silently un-own a privilege-bearing
# path — the dangerous version of this file is the one where someone adds
# `/modules/ @app-teams` and nobody notices bootstrap/ lost its owner too.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The only place privilege elevation is created: Spaces, the privileged stacks,
# and the role bindings that make them privileged.
/bootstrap/             @Jnesspace

# The code that runs *inside* those elevated stacks. Editing patterns/ is
# equivalent to editing what a Space-admin token executes.
/patterns/              @Jnesspace

# The server-side gates. Weakening a Rego rule is indistinguishable from
# removing the gate, and it does not look like a privileged change in a diff.
/policies/              @Jnesspace

# RBAC as code — who can trigger, confirm, and read.
/roles/                 @Jnesspace

# Where the elevated tokens actually run. Moving an engine onto a shared pool
# widens the token-theft surface without touching any policy.
/worker-pools/          @Jnesspace

# Scheduled runs and tasks execute with the stack's credentials on a timer, with
# no human present. A scheduled task is a remote shell that fires at 3am.
/schedules/             @Jnesspace

# Blueprints create stacks. A blueprint that targets a privileged Space vends
# privilege through the self-service form.
/blueprints/            @Jnesspace
/bootstrap/blueprints/  @Jnesspace

# CI is privilege-bearing too. Whoever can edit a workflow can run code in this
# repo's context, and — once any workflow holds a write permission — can often
# escalate from there. Same protection as bootstrap/.
/.github/               @Jnesspace

# The verification config. Relaxing a scanner rule deletes a check while looking
# like a lint tweak, and .gitignore is how a file stops being reviewed at all.
/.tflint.hcl            @Jnesspace
/.checkov.yaml          @Jnesspace
/.gitleaks.toml         @Jnesspace
/.yamllint.yaml         @Jnesspace
/.pre-commit-config.yaml @Jnesspace
/.gitignore             @Jnesspace
